 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed board changes.** Fast updates and independence from a realtime vendor outweigh the added connection-capacity and reconnect operations work. Five-second HTTPS polling offers simpler stateless operation and meets the freshness requirement, but adds repeated reads and seconds of delivery lag. A hosted realtime service reduces connection operations and speeds delivery development, but introduces recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets obligate the change store to support replayable revisions and reliable publication, delivery to manage sessions, and browser sync to reconnect and catch up. See [Board Change Delivery](decisions/board-change-delivery.md) for the criteria, alternatives, and accepted tradeoffs.
